  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nIssued 201 PM MST SAT NOV 7 2026\nA ridge of high pressure will build over the region through the end \nof the work week. This will bring generally warm and dry conditions \nwith above normal temperatures through Friday. Most locations will \nsee high temperatures about 10-15 degrees above normal, near record \ndaily highs. For example, Boise has a forecast high temperature of \n67F, with the daily record near 71F. By Friday, a deep trough will \nsplit and produce a cut off low pressure system near California, \nwith a strong atmospheric river poised to affect the OR/WA coast. \nSome of the cooler air and moisture from this atmospheric river will \ncreep into eastern Oregon and southern Idaho by next Saturday. This \nwill bring slightly cooler temperatures with valley rain showers and \nhigh elevation snow. Uncertainty remains in the track of the cut off \nlow and the strength of the atmospheric river which creates ample \nvariability in snow levels and forecast precipitation amounts. \nCurrent forecast shows temperatures creeping back 5 degrees above \nnormal with a 20-40% chance of measurable precipitation in southeast \nOR and southwest Idaho, with higher potential over terrain.",
